{
  name: CCC handling
  testpoints:
  [
    {
      name: ccc_getstatus
      desc:
        '''
        Reads GETSTATUS on both targets with no pending interrupt, then
        queues an IBI with handling disabled and checks the main target's
        vendor pending-IBI bit. Requires exact bytes and continuation/final
        target EOD on each response without controller-requested abort.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_getstatus"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_getstatus_unsupported_defining_byte
      desc:
        '''
        Sends GETSTATUS with defining bytes 0x00, 0x01, 0x90, 0x91, 0x92,
        0xE0, 0xFE, and 0xFF to both main and virtual targets with TE5
        detection disabled and enabled. Requires address NACKs, no new TE
        events, and preservation of a Protocol Error set by a real CCC
        parity error. Chains a Format 1 GETSTATUS without STOP, checks exact
        response bytes and target EOD on both targets, and verifies that the
        successful read clears Protocol Error without retaining the rejected
        defining byte.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_getstatus_unsupported_defining_byte"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_setdasa
      desc:
        '''
        The test sets dynamic address and virtual dynamic address by
        sending SETDASA CCC. Then it verifies that correct addresses have
        been set by reading STBY_CR_DEVICE_ADDR CSR.
        The test also sends a random number of CCCs targeting devices other
        than DUT, and checks if the dynamic address was not accepted.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_setdasa"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_setdasa_nack
      desc:
        '''
        The test sets dynamic address and virtual dynamic address by
        sending SETDASA CCC. Then it sends second SETDASA command and checks
        that targets NACKed them.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_setdasa_nack"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_setnewda
      desc:
        '''
        The test sets dynamic address and virtual dynamic address directly
        using CSR accesses. Then it sends SETNEWDA commands to both targets
        and checks their dynamic addresses got updated.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_setnewda"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_rstdaa
      desc:
        '''
        Sets dynamic address via STBY_CR_DEVICE_ADDR CSR, then sends
        RSTDAA CCC and verifies that the address got cleared.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_rstdaa"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_getbcr
      desc:
        '''
        Reads BCR register content by sending GETBCR CCC and examining
        returned data.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_getbcr"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_getdcr
      desc:
        '''
        Reads DCR register content by sending GETDCR CCC and examining
        returned data.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_getdcr"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_getmwl
      desc:
        '''
        Reads MWL register content by sending GETMWL CCC and examining
        returned data.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_getmwl"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_getmrl
      desc:
        '''
        Reads MRL using GETMRL. The main target advertises IBI payload and
        returns three bytes; the virtual target advertises no IBI payload
        and returns exactly two bytes. Every byte and continuation/final
        target EOD is checked without controller-requested abort.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_getmrl"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_setaasa
      desc:
        '''
        Issues the broadcast SETAASA CCC and checks if the target uses
        its static address as dynamic by examining STBY_CR_DEVICE_ADDR
        CSR.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_setaasa"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_setaasa_ignore
      desc:
        '''
        Assigns dynamic address different to static address using CSR.
        Issues the broadcast SETAASA CCC and checks if the target ignores
        this command by examining STBY_CR_DEVICE_ADDR CSR.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_setaasa_ignore"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_getpid
      desc:
        '''
        Sends the CCC to the target and examines if the returned PID
        matches the expected.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_getpid"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_enec_disec_direct
      desc:
        '''
        Sends DISEC CCC to the target and verifies that events are disabled.
        Then, sends ENEC CCC to the target and checks that events are enabled.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_enec_disec_direct"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_enec_disec_bcast
      desc:
        '''
        Sends broadcast DISEC CCC and verifies that events are disabled.
        Then, sends broadcast ENEC CCC and checks that events are enabled.
        Checks zero, individual IBI/CRR/HJ bits, reserved-only bits, and
        all-bits-set masks without changing unrelated event enables.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_enec_disec_bcast"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_setmwl_direct
      desc:
        '''
        Sends directed SETMWL CCC to the target and verifies that the
        register got correctly set. The check is performed by examining
        relevant wires in the target DUT.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_setmwl_direct"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_setmrl_direct
      desc:
        '''
        Randomly selects the main or virtual target for each directed SETMRL
        write. Main-target writes carry MRL and IBIL; virtual-target writes
        carry MRL only and preserve IBIL. Checks CSR values and capability-correct
        GETMRL readback from both targets after each write. The random selection
        does not guarantee both write formats are exercised in one invocation.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_setmrl_direct"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_setmwl_bcast
      desc:
        '''
        Sends broadcast SETMWL CCC and verifies that the
        register got correctly set. The check is performed by examining
        relevant wires in the target DUT.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_setmwl_bcast"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_setmrl_bcast
      desc:
        '''
        Sends SETMRL CCC and verifies that the
        register got correctly set. The check is performed by examining
        relevant wires in the target DUT.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_setmrl_bcast"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_rstact
      desc:
        '''
        Crosses broadcast/direct RSTACT and all three supported reset actions
        with an immediate reset pattern, STOP followed directly by a reset
        pattern, repeated START followed directly by a reset pattern, STOP
        followed by START, repeated START followed by another CCC, and repeated
        START followed by a private write.
        Direct RSTACT addresses both the main and virtual targets before ending.
        Every detected pattern retires its arm/action while preserving the
        configured request and existing default-reset escalation semantics.
        Checks every continuation address ACK, chained SETMWL/GETMWL payload,
        and private RX descriptor/data. Checks the arm and action after START
        while SCL remains high, then verifies the first SCL falling edge clears
        both. Samples the arm and action when the reset pattern is detected and
        checks both reset outputs without resending RSTACT. Every scenario starts
        with fresh reset-escalation state. A reset pattern after STOP must preserve
        the action through its final START/STOP, which has no intervening SCL
        falling edge. The explicit repeated START precedes the pattern body;
        it is additional to the pattern's own final repeated START and STOP.
        Holds the bus free during reset recovery and checks request persistence.
        Verifies peripheral_reset_done_i clears the peripheral request without
        clearing escalation, including held and deasserted acknowledgement.
        Peripheral reset covers both permitted integration models: acknowledgement
        with retained I3C state, and a second request followed by actual core reset
        with cleared state. NO_RESET retains state; whole-target reset requires
        actual core reset. Cleared-state recovery boots without dynamic addresses,
        rejects stale addresses, and assigns both targets through ENTDAA. Retained
        targets must not participate in ENTDAA. Every recovery path checks directed
        SETMWL/GETMWL and GETSTATUS on both targets, plus main-target private write
        descriptor/data and private read data. Checks no reset request is retriggered.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_rstact"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_direct_multiple_wr
      desc:
        '''
        Sends a sequence of multiple directed SETMWL CCCs. The first and
        the last have non-matching address. The two middle ones set MWL
        to different values. Checks the ACK/NACK sequence and the final MWL
        value from the second matching write. The first matching write's
        intermediate value is not observed.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_direct_multiple_wr"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_direct_multiple_rd
      desc:
        '''
        Sends SETMWL CCC. Then sends multiple directed GETMWL CCCs to
        thee different addresses. Only the one for the target should
        be ACK-ed with the correct MWL content.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_direct_multiple_rd"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_entdaa
      desc:
        '''
        Verifies ENTDAA procedure: controller issues ENTDAA CCC, target responds
        with the exact configured PID/BCR/DCR for each target, and the controller
        assigns dynamic addresses. Checks both address CSRs, subsequent CCCs,
        and exact private write/read data after the terminating STOP.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_entdaa"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_entdaa_arb_lost
      desc:
        '''
        Attempts to inject arbitration_lost_i during ENTDAA ID transmission,
        then checks main-target address validity and GETBCR responsiveness.
        Injection timeout is only logged; the test does not require observed
        LostArbitration entry or first-round loss followed by a successful retry.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_entdaa_arb_lost"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_entdaa_early_stop
      desc:
        '''
        Verifies ENTDAA with early STOP after assigning only the main target.
        The virtual target should remain unaddressed.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_entdaa_early_stop"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_entdaa_te3_te4
      desc:
        '''
        Injects TE3 assigned-address parity error and TE4 invalid reserved
        byte (7E/W instead of 7E/R) in separate single-slot ENTDAA transfers.
        Checks NACKs, error status, and exact error counts. Each transfer ends
        with STOP; same-frame TE3 retry and rejection of TE4 continuation
        before STOP are not exercised.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_entdaa_te3_te4"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_enthdr_all_codes
      desc:
        '''
        Verifies all ENTHDR codes (0x20-0x27) cause the target to enter HDR mode.
        Sr plus private R/W or 7E/W must not end that mode, and STOP alone must
        not exit HDR. After the HDR exit pattern, checks CCC and exact private
        write/read functionality. HDR payload transfer itself is unsupported.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_enthdr_all_codes"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_error_det_enable
      desc:
        '''
        Reads and logs TE0-TE5 detection-enable defaults, then toggles TE0 and
        TE5 enable bits with readback checks. Sends an unsupported CCC with
        TE0 disabled and checks that its error status remains clear.
        After re-enabling TE0, checks GETBCR responsiveness; no new error is
        injected to prove resumed detection.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_error_det_enable"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_getcaps
      desc:
        '''
        Tests GETCAPS (0x95) direct GET CCC with various defining bytes,
        targeting both main and virtual targets.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_getcaps"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_setdasa_padding_err
      desc:
        '''
        Verifies that SETDASA/SETNEWDA with padding bit[0]=1 triggers a framing
        error and does NOT apply the address.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_setdasa_padding_err"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_te2_parity
      desc:
        '''
        Verifies TE2 error detection: bad T-bit parity on CCC defining byte
        causes the target to abort the CCC and signal TE2.
        Each injection requires exactly one passive event. Sr plus main,
        reserved and virtual headers/data must produce no new field, CSR or
        FIFO effects until STOP; valid traffic then recovers.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_te2_parity"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_setdasa_direction_det_en
      desc:
        '''
        Sends wrong-direction SETDASA to each unassigned main/virtual static
        address with TE5 DET_EN=0 and 1. Requires NACK in both modes, exact
        per-operation event/counter/status deltas, and unchanged DA value/valid.
        Good write-direction assignments must subsequently ACK and commit.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_setdasa_direction_det_en"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_te5_wrong_direction
      desc:
        '''
        Checks the wrong R/W direction for all seven supported GET CCCs and
        all six write-only CCCs on both targets. SETDASA is exercised before
        dynamic address assignment. Each rejected transaction ends in STOP,
        then valid CCC traffic must work. RSTACT supports both directions
        and is covered by its format-specific tests.
        Assigns each target's dynamic address to its static address, then
        repeats SETDASA with both R/W directions and TE5 detection disabled
        and enabled. Correct-direction refusals must not report TE5;
        wrong-direction requests report exactly one event and counter
        increment with sticky status only when detection is enabled.
        All rejected requests must preserve the assigned addresses and
        their valid bits.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_te5_wrong_direction"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_unknown_broadcast
      desc:
        '''
        Unsupported broadcast CCCs must ACK 0x7E/W, ignore the command/payload,
        and recover at STOP or repeated START. Covers unknown opcodes, ENDXFER,
        and MLANE, including their defining bytes and optional payload.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_unknown_broadcast"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_unsupported_direct_nack
      desc:
        '''
        Verifies that unsupported direct CCCs are NACKed by the target.
        Covers ENDXFER and MLANE defining bytes for both writes and reads,
        alongside the existing unsupported direct CCCs. Also checks unsupported
        GETCAPS defining bytes 0x01, 0x92, 0x94, and 0xFF on both targets.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_unsupported_direct_nack"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_vendor_codes
      desc:
        '''
        Samples broadcast vendor CCCs with payload, checks NACKs for selected
        direct vendor CCCs, and checks subsequent GETBCR responsiveness.
        Does not check architectural state or FIFO effects to establish that
        broadcast vendor payloads were ignored without side effects.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_vendor_codes"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_abort_bcast_stop
      desc:
        '''
        Controller STOP during CCC at various phases:
        STOP during broadcast SET data (incomplete data).
        Verifies clean recovery.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_abort_bcast_stop"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_abort_get_sr
      desc:
        '''
        Controller Sr abort during multi-byte GET CCC reads. Reads fewer bytes
        than expected, then sends Sr to abort. Verifies clean recovery and
        correct subsequent CCC processing.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_abort_get_sr"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_addr_lifecycle
      desc:
        '''
        Boots without dynamic addresses, then checks SETDASA address/valid
        values, SETNEWDA address values, and RSTDAA clearing both address-valid
        bits. Tests main and virtual targets. There is no initial RSTDAA, and
        the final check does not inspect the cleared address values.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_addr_lifecycle"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_back_to_back
      desc:
        '''
        Back-to-back CCCs with no idle gap between transactions.
        Verifies all CCCs process correctly.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_back_to_back"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_chain_bcast
      desc:
        '''
        Chain multiple broadcast CCCs via Sr+7E/W within a single frame.
        Verifies each CCC takes effect.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_chain_bcast"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_handoff_acquire_stop
      desc:
        '''
        Uses real SCL/SDA traffic to send STOP after seven or eight ENEC
        command bits, before the command T-bit. Crosses two system-clock
        phases with 32 ns and 40 ns SCL-high intervals before STOP.
        Requires exactly one detected STOP per case and acquisition only
        after all eight command bits. Checks that unowned acquisition never
        coincides with STOP: command reception requires SCL rising, which
        excludes the trigger for the registered STOP event.
        An incomplete opcode produces no CCC handoff; a complete opcode
        produces CccDone from RxCmdTbit after ownership has transferred.
        Checks STOP-side-effect suppression, same-edge ownership/context
        retirement, and subsequent GETBCR recovery. Uses no internal overrides.
        Synthetic simultaneous CCC-valid/STOP remains an interface-level
        defensive check in the block ccc_complete_handoff test, not claimed
        as a reachable top-level transaction.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_handoff_acquire_stop"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_bcast_boundary
      desc:
        '''
        Crosses ENEC, DISEC, SETMWL, SETMRL, RSTDAA, SETAASA, and RSTACT
        with STOP, Sr plus another CCC, and immediate Sr plus private
        write/read. No STOP or extra 7E/W appears at the immediate private
        boundary. All seven supported ordinary broadcasts are covered.
        SETMRL covers both two-byte and three-byte capability formats, giving
        32 cases. The CCC-chain cases include ignored extension bytes.
        Checks each command's register/action/address effects before the next
        transaction, address ACK, exact private RX descriptor/data and queued
        TX data. Uses the address appropriate after RSTDAA/SETAASA.
        ENTDAA and ENTHDR use their separate mode-specific tests; they must not
        acquire the ordinary broadcast-to-private ending rule.
        Monitors same-edge ownership release, the address-resume qualifier,
        request cancellation, and command/defining-byte retirement.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_bcast_boundary", "ccc_bcast_boundary_no_ibi"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_rstact_terminator_address
      desc:
        '''
        Sends directed RSTACT with no addressed target, an unknown target,
        main-only/virtual-only writes, and reads. Crosses supported reset
        actions, VT identification, and an unsupported defining byte.
        Checks arm, action, and VT flag before and after the Sr+7E/W
        terminator, which must not execute a target-address side effect.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_rstact_terminator_address"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_rstact_pattern_consumption
      desc:
        '''
        NO_RESET RSTACT, broadcast/direct crossed with in-frame and
        STOP-before-first-pattern. C16-P1 observes armed action and no reset
        at first detection. C16-S checks immediate arm/action retirement.
        C16-P2 sends a second raw pattern with no intervening normal START,
        CCC, private transfer, rearm or external reset and requires only the
        default peripheral request. All three results are captured before
        the final assertion, even when retirement fails; missing detections
        fail explicitly. Raw START/Sr/STOP/SCL-fall/pending-clear events are
        logged for attribution, not inferred from the whole-test result.
        Separate controls exercise explicit DB0 rearm, completed START+address,
        retention before SCL falling, and VT flag independence.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_rstact_pattern_consumption"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_getstatus_complete_then_chain
      desc:
        '''
        Injects a protocol error and completes GETSTATUS without STOP.
        Checks immediate error clearing and a clear result from the next
        addressed GETSTATUS, both within the same command and after a new
        CCC header. Ends through 7E/W followed by private write/read.
        The clean private-write descriptor must not inherit the earlier
        CCC parity error; CCC errors remain visible through GETSTATUS.
        Checks exact two-byte responses and every continuation/final target
        EOD with no controller-requested abort. A later injected TE2 must
        occur exactly once and relatch Protocol Error after the earlier clear.
        Live-global-error descriptor isolation is a separate executable
        test_ccc_error_private_descriptor in test_te_errors, mapped there.
        The existing aborted-GETSTATUS chain test checks the opposite case:
        an incomplete read must preserve the error.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_getstatus_complete_then_chain"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_mrl_capability_boundaries
      desc:
        '''
        Crosses both local BCR[2] values with broadcast and directed SETMRL.
        Checks legal two-/three-byte endings followed by Sr plus another
        CCC and exact GETMRL bytes plus every continuation/final target EOD.
        Changes live BCR after
        command/address acceptance to prove the current message keeps its
        sampled capability. Two-byte writes preserve IBIL.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_mrl_capability_boundaries"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_incomplete_stop_recovery
      desc:
        '''
        Truncates broadcast and directed command T-bit, required defining
        byte/T-bit, and payload byte/T-bit, including SETMRL with a required
        third byte missing. Sends STOP, then checks context retirement and a
        working directed CCC. Does not require recovery at premature Sr.
        Separately, detected TE2 parity errors must ignore further target and
        reserved headers until STOP.
        Passive effect monitoring requires stable committed fields and no new
        field/CSR/FIFO writes in the STOP or TE2 quiescent windows. Earlier
        valid bytes are not required to roll back. Checker tasks are awaited.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_incomplete_stop_recovery"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_chain_direct
      desc:
        '''
        Covers every supported directed opcode: ENEC, DISEC, SETDASA, SETNEWDA,
        SETMWL, SETMRL, GETMWL, GETMRL, GETPID, GETBCR, GETDCR, GETSTATUS,
        GETCAPS, and RSTACT. Crosses 26 supported read/write/defining-byte
        formats with STOP and multi-CCC chaining (52 scenarios). Each command
        visits an unknown address and both local targets without losing its
        context. Checks every response byte/length and command side effect.
        The chain variant ignores extension bytes after supported writes,
        then uses Sr+7E/W to issue GETCAPS with and without its defining byte.
        This verifies both command and defining-byte retirement; a new direct
        opcode is never sent without the required reserved header.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_chain_direct"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_random_interleave
      desc:
        '''
        Random sequence of SET/GET CCCs to random targets. Stress tests the
        CCC FSM state machine with varied patterns.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_random_interleave"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_rstact_arm_clear_on_start
      desc:
        '''
        After arming whole-target reset, checks that completed private
        write/read transfers leave the arm/action cleared. Cancellation occurs
        on the first SCL falling edge following START, not START alone or Sr.
        Sends a reset pattern without reissuing RSTACT, so the default
        peripheral reset cannot be masked by another armed action.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_rstact_arm_clear_on_start"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_rstact_escalation_clear
      desc:
        '''
        First triggers an unarmed default peripheral reset and observes the
        escalation arm. Checks clearing by broadcast RSTACT, addressed direct
        RSTACT (including an unsupported defining byte), and GETSTATUS on
        either target. Unaddressed RSTACT/GETSTATUS and GETBCR must not clear it.
        A second reset pattern checks peripheral versus escalated request,
        without arming an action that could mask the escalation result.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_rstact_escalation_clear"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_rstact_read_action
      desc:
        '''
        Verifies RSTACT internal state after arming. The first SCL falling
        edge following a new START clears the arm/action, so a subsequent
        read in that frame returns 0x80 (not armed). START alone and Sr do
        not clear the action. Recovery-time reads return 0xFF.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_rstact_read_action"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_rstact_unsupported_db
      desc:
        '''
        Verifies RSTACT with unsupported defining bytes is NACKed.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_rstact_unsupported_db"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_rstact_vt_detect
      desc:
        '''
        Verifies RSTACT Defining Byte 0x04 (Virtual Target Detect) flag set/clear
        and Defining Byte 0x84 (Virtual Target Indication) behavior.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_rstact_vt_detect"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_rstact_vt_detect_no_reset_arm
      desc:
        '''
        Verifies DB=0x04 does not arm a reset action: default peripheral->escalation
        path still works after sending RSTACT with VT detect defining byte.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_rstact_vt_detect_no_reset_arm"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_setmwl_stop_during_data
      desc:
        '''
        STOP during the second SETMWL data byte must not publish a partial
        configuration value. Checks a subsequent complete SETMWL, GETBCR,
        and exact private write/read transfers. No invalid-Sr recovery is
        required or inferred. A continuously sampled, awaited checker forbids
        new field/CSR/FIFO effects and state changes across the STOP window.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_setmwl_stop_during_data"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_getstatus_abort_then_chain_setmwl
      desc:
        '''
        Verifies that aborting GETSTATUS mid-read and immediately chaining into
        another CCC (SETMWL) in the same bus frame does not corrupt the target
        state.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_getstatus_abort_then_chain_setmwl"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_getstatus_sr_abort_preserves_protocol_err
      desc:
        '''
        A valid controller read-abort after the first GETSTATUS byte preserves
        Protocol Error. The next full GETSTATUS must return that error bit and
        clear it only on successful response completion.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_getstatus_sr_abort_preserves_protocol_err"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_entdaa_virtual_only
      desc:
        '''
        Coverage: ccc.sv RxCmdTbit -> HandleVirtualTargetENTDAA.
        Boot with main target already addressed. Virtual target has no dynamic
        address. ENTDAA skips HandleTargetENTDAA and goes directly to
        HandleVirtualTargetENTDAA.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_entdaa_virtual_only"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_entdaa_main_only
      desc:
        '''
        Coverage: HandleTargetENTDAA -> WaitForENTDAAEnd (entdaa_needs_virt_addr=0).
        Boot with virtual target already addressed. Main target has no dynamic
        address. After main completes, FSM goes to WaitForENTDAAEnd.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_entdaa_main_only"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_entdaa_both_addressed
      desc:
        '''
        Coverage: RxCmdTbit -> WaitForENTDAAEnd.
        Boot with both targets already addressed. ENTDAA goes directly to
        WaitForENTDAAEnd since neither target needs an address.
        Sr plus private R/W or 7E/W must not release ENTDAA mode. After STOP,
        checks exact private write/read data and a working directed CCC.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_entdaa_both_addressed"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_te0_reserved_addr_direct
      desc:
        '''
        Coverage: Toggle is_te0_err_condition, te0_err_ccc (ccc.sv:769).
        Send a direct CCC where the target address phase uses 7E/R (reserved
        address with read bit). Triggers TE0 in TxTargetAddrAck.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_te0_reserved_addr_direct"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_direct_chain_7e_termination
      desc:
        '''
        Coverage: ccc.sv target_addr_matches_rsvd -> WaitCCC (CccNextCmd).
        After a direct GET CCC response, send Sr+7E/W instead of STOP. Triggers
        the reserved address path in TxTargetAddrAck, transitioning directly to WaitCCC
        for CCC chaining.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_direct_chain_7e_termination"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_all_det_en_toggle
      desc:
        '''
        Coverage: All *_det_en_i toggle coverage.
        For TE0, TE1, TE2, TE5: toggle the detection enable CSR, inject
        the error with det_en=0 (verify no error flag), then with det_en=1
        (verify error fires).
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_all_det_en_toggle"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_te2_direct_def_byte_tbit
      desc:
        '''
        Coverage: ccc.sv RxDirectDefByteTbit -> WaitForStop -> WaitCCC,
        WaitDirectRstart -> RxDirectDefByteTbit.
        Send a direct CCC with defining byte, then an extra data byte with bad
        T-bit parity before the repeated start for the target address.
        Requires exactly one event and no new effects through subsequent
        main/reserved/virtual headers and data until STOP.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_te2_direct_def_byte_tbit"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_direct_extra_data_before_addr
      desc:
        '''
        Coverage: ccc.sv WaitDirectRstart -> RxDirectDefByteTbit,
        RxDirectDefByteTbit -> WaitDirectRstart.
        Send a direct CCC with defining byte, then extra data bytes with good
        T-bit parity before the repeated start. Then complete the CCC normally.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_direct_extra_data_before_addr"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_te2_data_byte_direct_set
      desc:
        '''
        Coverage: RxDataTbit -> WaitForStop -> WaitCCC.
        Send a direct SET CCC (SETMWL) and inject T-bit parity error on the
        first data byte after the target ACKs the address.
        Requires exactly one event and no new effects until STOP, including
        Sr/main/reserved/virtual traffic. With DET_EN=0 the same bad parity
        must allow a complete MWL update without event/counter/status changes.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_te2_data_byte_direct_set"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_entdaa_te3_det_en_toggle
      desc:
        '''
        Coverage: Toggle te3_err_det_en_i in ccc_entdaa.
        Inject TE3 (bad address parity during ENTDAA) with te3_err_det_en=0
        then with te3_err_det_en=1.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_entdaa_te3_det_en_toggle"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_stop_mid_transfer
      desc:
        '''
        Requires detected STOP in WaitDirectRstart, RxDefByteOrBusCond and
        RxDefByte, followed in each case by completed GETBCR recovery.
        The passive handoff monitor requires all six ordered STOP observations,
        no arbitration loss, STOP-side-effect suppression and ownership retirement.
        D/E/F are not exercised. Legal read termination occurs at SDA handoff
        boundaries; this selector leaves active TxData/TxDataTbitCont/
        TxDataTbitEnd STOP arcs unproven. An RTL override is not coverage.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_stop_mid_transfer"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_entdaa_stop_in_waitstart
      desc:
        '''
        Coverage: ENTDAA FSM WaitStart -> Done.
        Issue ENTDAA CCC byte, then STOP immediately before Sr+7E/R.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_entdaa_stop_in_waitstart"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_entdaa_stop_in_sendidbit
      desc:
        '''
        Retains the original malformed ID-abort stimulus after eight ID bits;
        the reserved header must ACK, not silently skip the scenario.
        Requires one observed arbitration loss before STOP in ENTDAA WaitStart,
        then a two-address ENTDAA retry and GETBCR recovery.
        The passive handoff monitor requires all three STOP states and retirement.
        The legacy selector name does not imply SendIDBit-to-Done STOP coverage.
        Released-SDA ID-boundary termination without arbitration loss remains
        unproven; no additional experimental stimulus is added.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_entdaa_stop_in_sendidbit"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_entdaa_stop_in_receiveaddr
      desc:
        '''
        After an ACKed ENTDAA Sr+7E/R header and 64 ID bits, attempts STOP
        during the assigned-address byte and checks subsequent recovery.
        If the header NACKs, the test logs a warning and returns without
        exercising the scenario; a passing result alone does not establish
        ReceiveAddr STOP coverage.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_entdaa_stop_in_receiveaddr"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_entdaa_stop_in_ackrsvdbyte
      desc:
        '''
        Requires the reserved-header ACK, then retains the original malformed
        post-ACK STOP request. The passive monitor requires one arbitration
        loss followed by STOP in ENTDAA WaitStart, a two-address ENTDAA retry and
        GETBCR recovery, including all three STOP states and ownership retirement.
        Active ACK drives SDA low, so this is not legal active-AckRsvdByte STOP
        coverage. Pending-ACK dispatch termination is also not exercised.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_entdaa_stop_in_ackrsvdbyte"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_entdaa_stop_in_sendnack
      desc:
        '''
        Injects TE4 using 7E/W instead of 7E/R, then issues STOP after the
        NACK helper completes. Requires the NACK and exactly one TE4 event.
        The passive monitor requires STOP in ENTDAA WaitStop with no arbitration
        loss, then a two-address ENTDAA retry and GETBCR recovery with retirement
        at all three STOPs. Pending NACK dispatch before the ninth clock and
        active SendNack-to-Done STOP coverage remain unproven; no candidate
        dispatch stimulus is added.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_entdaa_stop_in_sendnack"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_stop_during_target_read
      desc:
        '''
        Retains malformed premature STOP requests A/B: A detects STOP after
        GETBCR completion in WaitForBusCond; B detects STOP in TxData with
        intentionally malformed/contention recovery, not a legal bus handoff.
        C requires final T=0 before STOP in WaitForBusCond. D requires first-byte
        T=1 before the helper's abort and STOP in RxTargetAddr.
        The passive monitor requires all eight attempt/recovery STOP states,
        STOP-side-effect suppression and ownership retirement. Arbitration
        losses are reported for malformed A/B; all other intervals require zero.
        Existing contention suppression is retained, not expanded or used as
        evidence of protocol legality. Active address-ACK/final-T/continuation-T
        STOP arcs remain unproven by this selector. Basic 1.1.1 section 5.1.2.3.4
        and FAQ Q23.5 limit STOP to the appropriate SDA ownership boundary.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_stop_during_target_read"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
    {
      name: ccc_csr_concurrent_stress
      desc:
        '''
        Stress test: concurrent random FW (AXI) and I3C CCC accesses to
        CCC-affected CSRs. FW randomly reads/writes CCC-related CSRs via AXI
        in the background while I3C performs 50 random supported CCC operations
        (GET and SET). GET CCCs verify ACK; SET CCCs send random valid data.
        Goal: expose data races, coherency bugs, or FSM hangs under concurrent
        access from both interfaces.
        '''
      tests: ["ccc_csr_concurrent_stress"]
      tags: ["top"]
    }
  ]
}
